course: dictionary
lesson: 8764
title: Formal words: severance
level: primary-6
stars: 3
explain: Severance is a formal word, the kind you meet in news reports, letters and work. Its main meaning is money paid to a worker when their job ends because the company no longer needs them. This money is also called severance pay or redundancy pay.
explain: Read the context: «Workers who were let go received severance pay.» Being let go means losing your job, and the pay comes with it, so severance must be that payment. Test your meaning by swapping it in: "The workers received money when their jobs ended" still makes sense, so you have it right.
explain: Severance can also mean ending a connection, as in «the severance of ties», where two people or groups stop being linked. It can also mean cutting something off. In every case, the clue is that something is being separated or split apart.
explain: Formal words can sound softer than the plain fact. Severance pay sounds gentler than "money for losing your job", but both mean the same thing. When you read formal language, ask yourself what is really happening underneath the careful words.
fact: Severance means money paid to a worker when their job ends because the company no longer needs them.
fact: «Severance pay» and «redundancy pay» mean the same thing.
fact: Severance can also mean ending a connection, as in «the severance of ties», or cutting something off.
fact: «Voluntary severance» means being paid to leave one's job.
fact: «Severance is 2 weeks' pay for each year worked.» To work out the amount, multiply 2 weeks by the number of years worked.
exercise: «Workers who were let go received severance pay.» What is severance pay? | *Money paid to a worker when their job ends because the company no longer needs them | Extra holiday days for hard work | A loan the worker must repay next month | A gift for a worker's birthday
exercise: «Severance is 2 weeks' pay for each year worked.» A worker has been there 5 years. How many weeks of severance pay do they get? | 7 weeks | 2 weeks | *10 weeks | 5 weeks
exercise: The two groups made a clean severance of ties. What does severance mean here? | Sending a letter | Building a bridge | Sharing a meal | *Ending a connection
test: «Voluntary severance» means what? | Being forced to stay in a job | A type of formal letter | *Being paid to leave one's job | Working extra hours for free
test: In «the severance of ties», what is being cut? | A pair of scissors | A piece of cloth | *A connection between people or groups | A rope or string
exam: Why might a news report say «workers received severance pay» rather than "workers got money for losing their jobs"? | *Formal wording sounds more measured, but the meaning is the same | It means the workers were not paid at all | It means a holiday bonus for good work | It is a type of tax on workers
